## 1. Introduction
This document outlines the design of a work-stealing task scheduler in Rust for CPU-bound parallel computation with dynamic Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) tasks. The scheduler will be designed to handle tasks with dependencies and ensure fairness among workers.

## 2. System Architecture
### 2.1. Worker Threads
The system will consist of multiple worker threads. Each worker thread will have its own thread pool to manage tasks.

### 2.2. Task Queue
A task queue will be used to store tasks. The queue will be a circular buffer to facilitate work-stealing.

### 2.3. Work Stealing
Workers will steal tasks from the queue when they are idle. The queue will be divided into segments, and each worker will steal tasks from its segment.

### 2.4. Task Dependencies
Tasks with dependencies will be stored in a separate data structure to ensure that dependencies are respected.

## 3. Data Structures

### 3.1. Task Queue
```rust
struct TaskQueue {
    buffer: Vec<Task>,
    head: usize,
    tail: usize,
    capacity: usize,
}
```

- `buffer`: A circular buffer to store tasks.
- `head`: The index of the next task to be dequeued.
- `tail`: The index of the next task to be enqueued.
- `capacity`: The maximum number of tasks the queue can hold.

### 3.2. Task
```rust
struct Task {
    id: usize,
    dependencies: Vec<usize>,
    body: Box<dyn FnOnce()>,
}
```

- `id`: A unique identifier for the task.
- `dependencies`: A list of task IDs that this task depends on.
- `body`: The body of the task, which is a closure.

### 3.3. Worker
```rust
struct Worker {
    id: usize,
    queue: Arc<Mutex<TaskQueue>>,
    tasks: Vec<Task>,
    dependencies: Vec<usize>,
}
```

- `id`: The unique identifier for the worker.
- `queue`: A shared reference to the task queue.
- `tasks`: A list of tasks assigned to the worker.
- `dependencies`: A list of task IDs that the worker depends on.

